  "qualification": [
    "An externally imposed zero RLIMIT_NOFILE previously caused production idle poll to return EINVAL and terminate the session. The fallback waits on the existing socket without allocating descriptors or changing the limit, then restores active-frame timeout.",
    "A Linux interposer reproduces the real kernel EINVAL and restores the limit before returning to the caller. The interim binary disconnects; the corrected binary continues serving authenticated requests.",
    "Each TV ran the exact ordinary worker bytes in its signed package through the private acceptance controller with production lifetime, while the installed managed worker remained ready.",
    "Each worker remained idle for 32 seconds at descriptor limit zero, served another request, then terminated within the 25-to-38-second window after a partial frame. The existing socket deadline or active-request watchdog may enforce termination (accepted guardian exit 1 or 137). A fresh child continued the remaining checks.",
    "Storage exhaustion and read-only faults were confined to a disposable 2 MiB tmpfs in a private namespace. Preserved intents/results and command witnesses verify recovery without replay.",
    "Acceptance used per-TV locks, no pending setup, model/runtime/signing pins, single-use operation records and an independent 300-second managed SDK rescue. Owned scopes/helpers/jobs were removed; rescue was disarmed and SDK loopback verified."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "This is bounded qualification of separate ordinary production worker instances, not an endurance run or selection of native as the service default.",
    "The limit-restoration race is deterministically qualified in the local Linux fixture; both TVs qualify zero-limit idle and active-frame deadlines without the interposer.",
    "Guardian/startup descriptor budgets, concurrent memory/process pressure, wider storage stalls, remaining lifecycle interruptions and production endurance remain open.",
    "Native-only capability entry and native-default rollout remain open. The .NET entry bridge, managed worker fallback and independent rescue remain deployed.",
    "No reboot, standby, global-policy, persistent file-capability or SMACK-rule change was performed."
  ]
